%[text] # Clinical Trial Site Selection Using Quantum Optimization
%[text] Select a fixed-size portfolio of synthetic clinical trial sites while balancing operational value, overlap between similar sites, regional mix, and hard incompatibilities. The workflow starts with a classical `intlinprog` baseline, then reformulates the same binary selection problem as a QUBO for Tabu search and QAOA. It can then submit the QAOA circuit to Amazon Web Services® (AWS®) for quantum simulation or to run on quantum hardware, which requires an AWS account (see Section 7 for details).
%[text] This is an operational planning example. It is not clinical decision support, patient eligibility logic, treatment recommendation, or evidence of clinical efficacy. The data is synthetic.
%[text] **Workflow:** synthetic site data -\> binary MILP baseline -\> QUBO formulation -\> Tabu search -\> QAOA -\> decoded portfolio comparison -\> hardware circuit inspection
%[text] **Requires:** Optimization Toolbox™, MATLAB® Support Package for Quantum Computing.
%[text] Copyright 2026 The MathWorks, Inc.
%%
%[text] ## 1. Synthetic Site Data
%[text] For this example, assume that we have already pre-screened the site choices to a final 8, and we must choose 4 of them. This smaller instance is intended for lower-cost hardware experiments: it uses one qubit per site, so reducing the candidate list from 16 to 8 cuts the QAOA circuit width in half and greatly reduces the number of pairwise cost rotations.
%[text] The synthetic `InvestigatorExpScore` represents study-specific investigator and site-team experience with the trial type, procedures, endpoints, or therapeutic area. This variable is intentionally strong for this example, so one feasible portfolio stands out clearly enough for a compact QAOA and AWS sampling demonstration.
%[text] `MapX` and `MapY` create a small synthetic sketch map. They have no units and are not latitude/longitude. They only let the example say "sites that are close together on this sketch are more likely to overlap in patient catchment, referral network, or startup resources."
siteRows = {
    "S01", "Northeast", 0.18, 0.78, 6.8, 7.0, 8.0, 6.5, 6.4, 2.1, 28
    "S02", "Northeast", 0.31, 0.83, 8.2, 5.0, 9.0, 6.0, 9.4, 2.8, 36
    "S03", "South",     0.64, 0.25, 8.5, 6.0, 8.4, 8.6, 9.5, 2.6, 38
    "S04", "South",     0.80, 0.33, 6.7, 8.0, 7.5, 8.7, 6.3, 1.9, 30
    "S05", "Midwest",   0.42, 0.49, 7.3, 7.0, 8.2, 6.9, 6.1, 2.2, 31
    "S06", "Midwest",   0.58, 0.56, 7.9, 5.0, 8.7, 6.6, 9.2, 2.7, 35
    "S07", "West",      0.16, 0.30, 8.1, 6.0, 8.3, 7.8, 9.3, 2.5, 34
    "S08", "West",      0.30, 0.22, 6.2, 8.0, 7.2, 8.5, 6.0, 1.8, 27
    };
sites = cell2table(siteRows, VariableNames=["SiteID","Region","MapX","MapY", ...
    "EnrollScore","StartupScore","QualityScore","AccessScore", ...
    "InvestigatorExpScore","Cost","Capacity"]);
operationalValue = 0.45*sites.EnrollScore + 0.20*sites.StartupScore + ...
    0.20*sites.QualityScore + 0.15*sites.AccessScore;
investigatorExpWeight = 1.5;
% Centering InvestigatorExpScore keeps the average site value on the
% original operational scale while rewarding above-average experience.
sites.Value = operationalValue + ...
    investigatorExpWeight*(sites.InvestigatorExpScore - mean(sites.InvestigatorExpScore));
sites = movevars(sites, "Value", Before="Cost");
selectedSiteCount = 4;
regionNames = ["Northeast","South","Midwest","West"];
regionTarget = [1 1 1 1];
overlapWeight = 4.0;
useConflicts = true;
conflictPairs = [1 3; 2 8; 4 6];
siteTableDisplay = sites(:,["SiteID","Region","InvestigatorExpScore","Value","Cost","Capacity"]) %[output:4cc0dc60]
%%
%[text] ## Site Overlap Model
%[text] Sites close together in the synthetic map have higher pairwise overlap. Replace this matrix with a domain-specific overlap measure when adapting the template.
dx = sites.MapX - sites.MapX.';
dy = sites.MapY - sites.MapY.';
overlapLengthScale = 0.18;
overlap = exp(-(dx.^2 + dy.^2)./(2*overlapLengthScale^2));
overlap(1:height(sites)+1:end) = 0;
plotSiteMap(sites, overlap, [], string.empty(1,0)) %[output:97791f71]
%%
%[text] ## 2. Classical Baseline
%[text] The classical model uses binary variables `x_i` for site selection and auxiliary variables `y_ij = x_i*x_j` for selected site pairs. `intlinprog` maximizes value minus overlap while enforcing site count, regional mix, and optional incompatibility constraints.
[xILP, ilpMetrics, exitflag] = solveSiteMilp(sites, overlap, overlapWeight, ...
    selectedSiteCount, regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs, useConflicts);
classicalComparison = summaryTable("intlinprog", ilpMetrics, regionNames) %[output:8072dcb1]
%%
%[text] ## 3. QUBO Formulation
%[text] A QUBO solver minimizes an unconstrained binary energy, usually written as `x'*Q*x` for a binary vector `x`. While the classical model above has explicit constraints, a QUBO does not. 
%[text] The QUBO formulation task is to keep the same binary decision variables, move the business objective into `Q`, and replace each required constraint with a penalty term.
%[text] The first tradeoff is sign - the site model maximizes net score, but a QUBO solver minimizes energy. If site `i` is selected, then `x_i = 1`; if not, `x_i = 0`. Putting `-Value_i` on the diagonal makes a valuable selected site lower the energy, because the contribution is `-Value_i*x_i^2`, and `x_i^2 = x_i` for binary variables.
%[text] Pair costs work differently. If selected sites `i` and `j` overlap, the model should add a positive cost `c*x_i*x_j`. In `x'*Q*x`, a symmetric matrix contributes both `Q(i,j)*x_i*x_j` and `Q(j,i)*x_j*x_i`. To add the pair cost once, store `c/2` in each symmetric entry.
numSites = height(sites);
Q = diag(-sites.Value);
[pairI, pairJ] = find(triu(true(numSites), 1));
for p = 1:numel(pairI)
    i = pairI(p);
    j = pairJ(p);
    pairCost = overlapWeight*overlap(i,j);
    Q(i,j) = Q(i,j) + pairCost/2;
    Q(j,i) = Q(j,i) + pairCost/2;
end
%%
%[text] The second tradeoff is penalty sizing. A penalty that is too small can make an infeasible bit string look attractive. A penalty that is much larger than the business objective can make objective differences hard for a heuristic or quantum algorithm to use. These values are individually tunable. A one-time penalty sweep for this synthetic data showed that `4` preserves the classical ground state while avoiding unnecessarily large QUBO coefficients.
countPenalty = 4.0;    % Penalty for choosing other than selectedSiteCount sites
regionPenalty = 4.0;   % Penalty for missing the target number of sites in a region
conflictPenalty = 4.0; % Penalty for selecting both sites in a conflict pair
%%
%[text] The count requirement is encoded as `countPenalty*(sum(x) - selectedSiteCount)^2`. Since `x_i^2 = x_i` for binary variables, this contributes linear terms on the diagonal and pair terms off the diagonal. The constant term is omitted because it shifts every QUBO energy by the same amount and cannot change the minimizer.
Q(1:numSites+1:end) = Q(1:numSites+1:end) + ...
    countPenalty*(1 - 2*selectedSiteCount);
for p = 1:numel(pairI)
    i = pairI(p);
    j = pairJ(p);
    Q(i,j) = Q(i,j) + countPenalty;
    Q(j,i) = Q(j,i) + countPenalty;
end
%%
%[text] The regional mix uses the same squared-penalty pattern, applied separately within each region. This keeps the QUBO compact because it does not introduce auxiliary variables.
siteRegion = string(sites.Region);
for r = 1:numel(regionNames)
    idx = find(siteRegion == regionNames(r));
    diagIdx = sub2ind([numSites numSites], idx, idx);
    Q(diagIdx) = Q(diagIdx) + regionPenalty*(1 - 2*regionTarget(r));
    for a = 1:numel(idx)-1
        for b = a+1:numel(idx)
            i = idx(a);
            j = idx(b);
            Q(i,j) = Q(i,j) + regionPenalty;
            Q(j,i) = Q(j,i) + regionPenalty;
        end
    end
end
%%
%[text] Conflict pairs are already pairwise conditions, so they enter as direct pair penalties. If a conflict is truly hard, choose a penalty large enough that violating it cannot be compensated by extra site value.
if useConflicts
    for e = 1:size(conflictPairs,1)
        i = conflictPairs(e,1);
        j = conflictPairs(e,2);
        Q(i,j) = Q(i,j) + conflictPenalty/2;
        Q(j,i) = Q(j,i) + conflictPenalty/2;
    end
end
Q = (Q + Q')/2;
qprob = qubo(Q);
imagesc(Q) %[output:8f4e89d4]
axis square %[output:8f4e89d4]
colorbar %[output:8f4e89d4]
title("QUBO Matrix for Clinical Site Selection") %[output:8f4e89d4]
xlabel("Site variable") %[output:8f4e89d4]
ylabel("Site variable") %[output:8f4e89d4]
%%
%[text] The QUBO energy rewards site value on the diagonal, penalizes selected site pairs with high overlap, and adds quadratic penalties for count, regional mix, and conflicts.
quboEnergyAtClassicalSolution = evaluateObjective(qprob, xILP) %[output:6bbba1b0]
%%
%[text] ## 4. Solve QUBO with Tabu Search
%[text] Before going quantum, verify the QUBO formulation with a classical QUBO heuristic. `tabuSearch` works on the same unconstrained `qubo` object and gives a quick check that the penalty model still returns a feasible portfolio with the expected score.
tabuResult = solve(qprob, Algorithm=tabuSearch(MaxTime=5, MaxIterations=200000, Display="off"));
xTabu = round(tabuResult.BestX(:));
tabuMetrics = scorePortfolio(xTabu, sites, overlap, overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, ...
    regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs);
tabuSummary = summaryTable("Tabu search", tabuMetrics, regionNames) %[output:3d11a366]
%%
%[text] ## 5. Quantum Solution - QAOA
%[text] Now solve the same QUBO using the **Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm**.
%[text] QAOA is a hybrid quantum-classical algorithm. The quantum part is a parameterized circuit that prepares and measures candidate bit strings for the QUBO. The classical part adjusts the circuit angles so that measured bit strings tend to have lower QUBO energy.
%[text] In this example, MATLAB simulates the QAOA circuit locally. 
%[text] - `qaoaNumLayers=2` uses two cost-mixer layer pairs
%[text] - `qaoaMaxFunctionEvaluations` is the maximum number of times the classical optimizer updates the quantum circuit's parameters (angles) to find better solutions
%[text] - `qaoaNumShots` is how many times the quantum computer runs the hybrid circuit. Because quantum results are probabilistic, more shots give you a clearer picture of the best answer. \
rng(2, "twister")
qaoaNumLayers = 2;
qaoaNumShots = 1000;
qaoaMaxFunctionEvaluations = 250;
qaoaOptions = optimset("MaxFunEvals", qaoaMaxFunctionEvaluations, "Display", "off");
qaoaResult = solve(qprob, Algorithm=qaoa(NumLayers=qaoaNumLayers, NumShots=qaoaNumShots, ...
    OptimizationSolverOptions=qaoaOptions));
xQAOA = round(qaoaResult.BestX(:));
qaoaMetrics = scorePortfolio(xQAOA, sites, overlap, overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, ...
    regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs);
qaoaSummary = summaryTable("QAOA", qaoaMetrics, regionNames) %[output:74001a5e]
%%
%[text] ## 6. Validation and Engineering Comparison
%[text] Compare the classical baseline, classical QUBO heuristic, and QAOA result in the original site-selection terms. The goal is not just a low QUBO energy; the decoded bit string must also satisfy the site count, regional mix, and conflict constraints.
solverComparison = summaryTable(["intlinprog";"Tabu search";"QAOA"], ... %[output:group:1836c9eb] %[output:4abe5629]
    [ilpMetrics, tabuMetrics, qaoaMetrics], regionNames) %[output:group:1836c9eb] %[output:4abe5629]
%%
%[text] Outlines show the portfolios returned by `intlinprog`, Tabu search, and QAOA. Each bit string is decoded back to site IDs and checked against the original constraints before interpretation.
plotSiteMap(sites, overlap, [xILP, xTabu, xQAOA], ["intlinprog","Tabu","QAOA"]) %[output:2da61127]
%%
%[text] ## 7. Run the QAOA Circuit on AWS Braket
%[text] Running on AWS Braket® from MATLAB requires some setup, as described in the [documentation](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/math/run-quantum-circuit-on-hardware-using-AWS.html). This example loads the environment variables that contain your AWS credentials from a local file "awsConfig.env". If you want to skip AWS and load previously-run results, set RUN\_ON\_AWS to "false".
%[text] In Section 5, MATLAB simulated the hybrid quantum-classical optimization loop: the quantum circuit was simulated many times while a classical optimizer tuned the QAOA angles. The circuit returned in that result object is a fixed, optimized `quantumCircuit` with those tuned angles built in. The AWS Braket step below does **not** rerun the QAOA hybrid loop on the AWS backend. Here, we save time on the hardware by submitting the already-optimized circuit and sampling bit strings from that circuit.
%[text] Choose one AWS backend profile before preparing the circuit for submission. 
%[text] - SV1 simulation is the default, for verifying the AWS workflow
%[text] - IonQ Forte® 1 is a sensible hardware choice for this dense QAOA circuit since it offers all-to-all connectivity, but it is substantially more expensive - be sure to check awsNumShots and price-per-shot
%[text] - Rigetti Cepheus™ is a lower-cost hardware pipeline check; for this formulation, expect a broad distribution and do not expect it to match the SV1 result reliably \
%[text] Each profile contains the settings and circuit adaptations for that backend - the device name, AWS region, and circuit-preparation function. 
%[text] Set whether to run on AWS - if "true" then also set your S3 path, the number of shots you want to run, and the timeout period.
% Set RUN_ON_AWS to "true" when you are ready to submit a task to AWS.
% When RUN_ON_AWS is "false", use offlineResultsFile if set
setenv("RUN_ON_AWS", "false");
loadenv("awsConfig.env");
offlineResultsFile = "./results-rigetti.mat"; % replay data used when RUN_ON_AWS is "false"
saveResultsFile = "./latest_results.mat"; % set to a .mat filename to save live AWS output for later replay
awsS3Path = "s3://<your-aws-bucket>/<project-directory>";
aws = awsbraket.rigettiCepheus(); % Load device and its settings. Choose from sv1(), ionqForte1(), or rigettiCepheus()
awsNumShots = qaoaNumShots; % Check the selected backend's shot limits and price-per-shot before running on hardware
awsWaitTimeoutSeconds = 15*60; % QPU queues can be long; increase this if you want MATLAB to keep waiting
setenv("AWS_REGION", aws.Region);
setenv("AWS_DEFAULT_REGION", aws.Region);
%%
%[text] The number of qubits equals the number of binary site-selection variables. The gate count gives an initial resource check; a backend must support enough qubits, enough circuit depth, and enough shots to estimate useful bit-string probabilities.
%[text] Some QPUs impose additional validation rules on gate parameters or compile dense QAOA circuits into much deeper native circuits. The selected backend profile handles any circuit adaptation needed before submission. For example, the IonQ Forte 1 profile bounds rotation angles, while the SV1 and Rigetti Cepheus profiles submit the circuit unchanged. 
siteQAOACircuit = qaoaResult.AlgorithmResult.AlgorithmInformation.Circuit;
plot(siteQAOACircuit) %[output:795be263]
%%
%[text] `submittedCircuit` is the circuit that will be sent to AWS. It may be identical to the local QAOA circuit or a backend-compatible equivalent, depending on the selected profile.
submittedCircuit = aws.prepareCircuit(siteQAOACircuit);
qaoaCircuitResources = table(siteQAOACircuit.NumQubits, ... %[output:group:8c74fc72] %[output:7e6999bc]
    numel(siteQAOACircuit.Gates), numel(submittedCircuit.Gates), ... %[output:7e6999bc]
    VariableNames=["NumQubits","QAOAGates","SubmittedGates"]) %[output:group:8c74fc72] %[output:7e6999bc]
%%
%[text] The task submission creates a `QuantumDeviceAWS` object, inspects the device, runs the circuit, waits for completion, and fetches measured bit strings.
%[text] `fetchOutput` returns a `quantum.gate.QuantumMeasurement` object with measured states, counts, and probabilities in a consistent MATLAB format.
%[text] If you want an offline replay file after a successful AWS run, set `saveResultsFile` to a MAT-file name and the script saves the fetched measurement object.
clear awsMeasurement;
awsMeasurements = table(strings(0,1), zeros(0,1), zeros(0,1), ...
    VariableNames=["State","Count","Probability"]);
runOnAWS = ~strcmpi(getenv("RUN_ON_AWS"), "false");
if runOnAWS %[output:group:743c23f6]
    fprintf("Running %d shots on AWS %s with timeout=%d\n", awsNumShots, aws.DeviceName, awsWaitTimeoutSeconds);
    awsDevice = quantum.backend.QuantumDeviceAWS(aws.DeviceName, Region=aws.Region, S3Path=awsS3Path);
    fetchDetails(awsDevice)
    awsTask = run(submittedCircuit, awsDevice, NumShots=awsNumShots);
    taskFinished = wait(awsTask, "finished", awsWaitTimeoutSeconds);
    awsTaskStatus = string(awsTask.Status)
    if ~taskFinished
        awsStatus = "AWS task " + string(awsTask.TaskARN) + " is still " + awsTaskStatus + ...
            " after " + string(awsWaitTimeoutSeconds) + " seconds. Wait longer or fetch the same task later."
        warning("ClinicalTrialSiteSelection:AWSTaskNotFinished", "%s", char(awsStatus))
    elseif awsTaskStatus == "failed"
        error("ClinicalTrialSiteSelection:AWSTaskFailed", ...
            "AWS task %s failed. Inspect fetchDetails(awsTask) or the AWS Braket console for the backend failure reason.", ...
            string(awsTask.TaskARN))
    else
        awsMeasurement = fetchOutput(awsTask);
        awsMeasurements = awsbraket.measurementToTable(awsMeasurement);
        if strlength(saveResultsFile) > 0
            save(saveResultsFile, "awsMeasurement", "awsMeasurements")
        end
    end
elseif strlength(offlineResultsFile) > 0 && isfile(offlineResultsFile)
    fprintf("Using previously-run results from %s\n", offlineResultsFile); %[output:7da2d2d5]
    [awsMeasurements, awsMeasurement] = awsbraket.loadSavedResults(offlineResultsFile);
else
    fprintf("runOnAWS set to false and no previous results are available - generating empty table\n");
end %[output:group:743c23f6]
%%
%[text] AWS Braket returns measured qubits in the order reported by the task result. This example uses `q[0]` as site 1, `q[1]` as site 2, and so on, matching the circuit emitted by `solve`. The measured bit strings are decoded back into site portfolios and scored in the original problem terms.
if ~isempty(awsMeasurements) %[output:group:5238d5fe]
    awsCandidates = scoreSiteSelectionStates(awsMeasurements, qprob, sites, overlap, ...
        overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs);
    feasibleAWSCandidates = awsCandidates(awsCandidates.Feasible, :);
    feasibleAWSCandidates = sortrows(feasibleAWSCandidates, "NetScore", "descend");
    if isempty(feasibleAWSCandidates)
        awsFeasibilityNote = "No feasible portfolio was observed. Increase NumShots, or rerun the local QAOA optimization with more tuning before submitting a new fixed circuit."
    else
        bestFeasibleAWSCandidates = feasibleAWSCandidates(1:min(5, height(feasibleAWSCandidates)), :) %[output:7eb61424]
    end
else
    if ~exist("awsStatus", "var")
        awsStatus = "AWS was skipped and no offline result file was found. Set RUN_ON_AWS=true to submit a task, or set offlineResultsFile to a MAT-file saved from this same circuit."
    end
end %[output:group:5238d5fe]
%%
%[text] ## Local Functions
%%
function [xBest, metrics, exitflag] = solveSiteMilp(sites, overlap, overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs, useConflicts)
    N = height(sites);
    [pairI, pairJ] = find(triu(true(N), 1));
    numPairs = numel(pairI);
    numVars = N + numPairs;
    f = zeros(numVars, 1);
    f(1:N) = -sites.Value;
    f(N+1:end) = overlapWeight*overlap(sub2ind([N N], pairI, pairJ));
    Aeq = zeros(1 + numel(regionNames), numVars);
    beq = [selectedSiteCount; regionTarget(:)];
    Aeq(1, 1:N) = 1;
    for r = 1:numel(regionNames)
        Aeq(1+r, string(sites.Region) == regionNames(r)) = 1;
    end
    if ~useConflicts
        conflictPairs = zeros(0,2);
    end
    A = zeros(3*numPairs + size(conflictPairs,1), numVars);
    b = zeros(size(A,1), 1);
    row = 0;
    for p = 1:numPairs
        i = pairI(p); j = pairJ(p); y = N + p;
        row = row + 1; A(row, y) = 1; A(row, i) = -1;
        row = row + 1; A(row, y) = 1; A(row, j) = -1;
        row = row + 1; A(row, i) = 1; A(row, j) = 1; A(row, y) = -1; b(row) = 1;
    end
    for e = 1:size(conflictPairs,1)
        row = row + 1;
        A(row, conflictPairs(e,1)) = 1;
        A(row, conflictPairs(e,2)) = 1;
        b(row) = 1;
    end
    opts = optimoptions("intlinprog", Display="off");
    [z, ~, exitflag] = intlinprog(f, 1:numVars, A, b, Aeq, beq, ...
        zeros(numVars,1), ones(numVars,1), opts);
    xBest = round(z(1:N));
    metrics = scorePortfolio(xBest, sites, overlap, overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, ...
        regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs);
end
function metrics = scorePortfolio(x, sites, overlap, overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs)
    x = double(x(:) > 0.5);
    selected = logical(x);
    regionCounts = zeros(1, numel(regionNames));
    for r = 1:numel(regionNames)
        regionCounts(r) = nnz(selected & string(sites.Region) == regionNames(r));
    end
    overlapPenalty = 0;
    for i = 1:numel(x)-1
        for j = i+1:numel(x)
            overlapPenalty = overlapPenalty + overlapWeight*overlap(i,j)*x(i)*x(j);
        end
    end
    conflictViolations = 0;
    for e = 1:size(conflictPairs,1)
        conflictViolations = conflictViolations + double(x(conflictPairs(e,1)) && x(conflictPairs(e,2)));
    end
    metrics.SelectedX = x;
    metrics.SelectedSiteIDs = join(string(sites.SiteID(selected)).', ", ");
    metrics.SelectedCount = nnz(selected);
    metrics.RegionCounts = regionCounts;
    metrics.CountViolations = abs(nnz(selected) - selectedSiteCount);
    metrics.RegionViolations = sum(abs(regionCounts - regionTarget));
    metrics.ConflictViolations = conflictViolations;
    metrics.TotalValue = sites.Value.'*x;
    metrics.OverlapPenalty = overlapPenalty;
    metrics.NetScore = metrics.TotalValue - overlapPenalty;
    metrics.TotalCost = sum(sites.Cost(selected));
    metrics.TotalCapacity = sum(sites.Capacity(selected));
    metrics.Feasible = metrics.CountViolations == 0 && metrics.RegionViolations == 0 && conflictViolations == 0;
end
function T = summaryTable(methods, metrics, regionNames)
    n = numel(metrics);
    selectedSites = strings(n,1);
    regionMix = strings(n,1);
    feasible = false(n,1);
    netScore = zeros(n,1);
    totalCost = zeros(n,1);
    totalCapacity = zeros(n,1);
    for k = 1:n
        selectedSites(k) = metrics(k).SelectedSiteIDs;
        regionMix(k) = join(regionNames(:) + "=" + string(metrics(k).RegionCounts(:)), ", ");
        feasible(k) = metrics(k).Feasible;
        netScore(k) = metrics(k).NetScore;
        totalCost(k) = metrics(k).TotalCost;
        totalCapacity(k) = metrics(k).TotalCapacity;
    end
    T = table(methods(:), selectedSites, regionMix, feasible, netScore, totalCost, ...
        totalCapacity, VariableNames=["Method","SelectedSites","RegionMix", ...
        "Feasible","NetScore","TotalCost","TotalCapacity"]);
end
function scoredStates = scoreSiteSelectionStates(measurementTable, qprob, sites, overlap, ...
    overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs)
%scoreSiteSelectionStates Decode measured bit strings and score portfolios.
    numStates = height(measurementTable);
    QUBOEnergy = zeros(numStates, 1);
    Feasible = false(numStates, 1);
    NetScore = zeros(numStates, 1);
    SelectedCount = zeros(numStates, 1);
    RegionMix = strings(numStates, 1);
    ConflictViolations = zeros(numStates, 1);
    SelectedSites = strings(numStates, 1);
    for row = 1:numStates
        x = stateToSiteVector(measurementTable.State(row), height(sites));
        metrics = scorePortfolio(x, sites, overlap, overlapWeight, selectedSiteCount, ...
            regionNames, regionTarget, conflictPairs);
        QUBOEnergy(row) = evaluateObjective(qprob, x);
        Feasible(row) = metrics.Feasible;
        NetScore(row) = metrics.NetScore;
        SelectedCount(row) = metrics.SelectedCount;
        RegionMix(row) = join(regionNames(:) + "=" + string(metrics.RegionCounts(:)), ", ");
        ConflictViolations(row) = metrics.ConflictViolations;
        SelectedSites(row) = metrics.SelectedSiteIDs;
    end
    scoredStates = [measurementTable, table(QUBOEnergy, Feasible, NetScore, ...
        SelectedCount, RegionMix, ConflictViolations, SelectedSites)];
end
function x = stateToSiteVector(state, numSites)
%stateToSiteVector Convert one measured bit string to a site-selection vector.
    bits = char(strip(string(state)));
    if numel(bits) ~= numSites
        error("ClinicalTrialSiteSelection:StateLengthMismatch", ...
            "State %s has %d bits, but the site-selection model has %d variables.", ...
            char(string(state)), numel(bits), numSites)
    end
    x = double(bits(:) == '1');
end
function plotSiteMap(sites, overlap, selections, solverNames)
    ax = gca;
    if ~isa(ax, 'matlab.graphics.axis.Axes')
        clf
        ax = axes;
    end
    cla(ax, "reset")
    hold(ax, "on")
    [row, col] = find(triu(overlap > 0.55, 1));
    for k = 1:numel(row)
        plot(ax, [sites.MapX(row(k)), sites.MapX(col(k))], ...
            [sites.MapY(row(k)), sites.MapY(col(k))], Color=[0.75 0.75 0.75], ...
            HandleVisibility="off")
    end
    regionNames = unique(string(sites.Region), "stable");
    colors = lines(numel(regionNames));
    markerSize = 70 + 100*(sites.Value - min(sites.Value))/(max(sites.Value) - min(sites.Value));
    for r = 1:numel(regionNames)
        idx = string(sites.Region) == regionNames(r);
        scatter(ax, sites.MapX(idx), sites.MapY(idx), markerSize(idx), colors(r,:), ...
            "filled", MarkerFaceAlpha=0.75, DisplayName=regionNames(r))
    end
    for k = 1:height(sites)
        text(ax, sites.MapX(k), sites.MapY(k), sites.SiteID(k), ...
            HorizontalAlignment="center", VerticalAlignment="middle", FontSize=8)
    end
    if ~isempty(selections)
        markers = ["o","s","^"];
        for s = 1:size(selections,2)
            idx = logical(selections(:,s));
            scatter(ax, sites.MapX(idx), sites.MapY(idx), 180, Marker=markers(s), ...
                MarkerFaceColor="none", MarkerEdgeColor="k", LineWidth=1.8, ...
                DisplayName=solverNames(s))
        end
    end
    hold(ax, "off")
    axis(ax, "equal")
    xlim(ax, [0 1])
    ylim(ax, [0 1])
    grid(ax, "on")
    xlabel(ax, "Synthetic map x")
    ylabel(ax, "Synthetic map y")
    title(ax, "Synthetic Candidate Sites")
    legend(ax, Location="bestoutside")
end

